{"text": "Walk, Roger A.\nWednesday, November 19, 2002 10:04 AM\nTo: Max Eisenberg (E-mail)\nSubject: Response to Endovasc recent press release\n\nDear Max,\n\n\u2018Could you please check whether the drafted statements for Philip Mortis USA are correct as they relate to those parts of\nthe ERP process that your organization handles?\n\nThank you in advance.\nRoger\nDRAFT\n\n\u201cThank you for your recent inquiry regarding a press release issued by Endovasc on November 6, 2002.\nPhilip Morris U.S.A. did not review the Endovasc press release prior to release and had no input into the\ncontent of the press release.\n\n\u2018The press release refers to grants provided under the Philip Morris External Research Program. The Philip\n\u2018Morris External Research Program supports the highest quality research that contributes to our fundamental\nscientific knowledge, helps address the concerns of the public health community regarding cigarette\nsmoking, and enables Philip Morris to continue its pursuit of product modification (s) or new product design\n() that might reduce the health risk of smoking.\n\nIn March 2001, Philip Morris U.S.A. notified Endovasc that it would provide a grant under this program to\nsupport the first year of a two-year research proposal, \u201cDevelopment and evaluation of therapeutic delivery\nsystems for administration of nicotine (NRA) in vasculat diseases\u201d. NRA stands for nicotine receptor\nagonist.\n\nIn September 2002, Philip Morris U.S.A. amended its External Research Program agreement with Endovasc\nto provide funding for the second year of the research proposal.\n\nThe scope of our funding is focused on the effects of smoking and also on the understanding of the\n\u2018mechanism of smoking related cardiovascular diseases (Where this project fits) but does not support\nresearch for trademarked products such as Angiogenix \u2122. Angiogenix \u2122 was never mentioned in the\nproposal or progress reports received from Endovasc. The exact formulation of Angiogenix \u2122 or its\nsimilarity (if any) with nicotine was never discussed with us.\n\n\u2018The original objective of the funded research as outlined in the proposal: \u201cAims: Itis\nthe specific aim of this grant proposal to develop site-directed delivery systems,\nwhich administer extremely low, therapeutic levels of nicotine receptor agonist (NRA) to ischemic areas\nover extended petiods of time. We wish to compare the effects achieved by administration of (a) nicotine-\nloaded liposomes and (b) NRA-coated stent implants to results achieved by oral administration of NRA in\nappropriate animal models...\u201d\n\nPhilip Morris U.S.A. is not involved in this research beyond providing the research grant and monitoring the\nprogress reports to ensure that the research is consistent with the proposal. Recipients of funding under this\nprogram are strongly encouraged to publish results of research in the open scientific literature. The results\nof this research belong entirely to the researchers, and publication does not require or involve review or\napproval from Philip Morris. Publications (articles, posters, and oral presentations) are required by contract\n\n2067227225",
